            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attack hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** The bathroom exhaust fan on the 2nd floor has alot of leakage through it (I connected you &amp; Tom Gross to talk about how to retrofit bathroom fans)



** All of the exterior wall plugs &amp; switches have much air leakage around &amp; through them (I emailed you some information about how to fix this)



** The older windows throughout the house, especially on the 2nd floor



** Around the small access door from your daughter's bedroom into the attic space above the kitchen



** The baseboard trim around the 2nd floor bedrooms



** The current chimney for the furnace and hot water tank



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house and also address the air leakage issues in the list above, you will likely achieve a 20% reduction in air leakage, you could potentially save $ 401 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 R20, and you mentioned that you may be interested in updating the siding in conjunction with upgrading all of the windows in your house. This would be a great time to add some additional exterior wall insulation. The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019, can help you cover the cost of this additional wall insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+), and I have modeled adding R10 (perhaps 2" of extruded polystyrene foam board insulation) to all of your exterior walls - even extending down of the pony wall section of the foundation walls (and there is a separate rebate for the foundation walls - so see that section of my report).



Savings: By adding R10 to all of the exterior walls of your house, you could potentially save $ 467 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The basement walls include a combination of lower concrete &amp; upper pony walls. The lower concrete section appears to be strapped with 2x3 lumber &amp; perhaps R8 fibreglass batts. The upper pony walls appear to have R20 and I think another R20 on the inside to bring the inside of the wall in-line with the lower concrete walls (this allows for the interior drywall to become 1 flat surface).



If you proceed to add a minimum R10 wall insulation on the main &amp; 2nd floors of your house, this extra wall insulation will naturally transition down to cover the exterior of the foundation pony walls as well.



Savings: By adding a minimum R10 to the exterior of the foundation pony walls, you could potentially save $ 85 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

There are a few windows &amp; the garden door in the kitchen &amp; dining room that have previously been updated, however the rest of the windows in the basement, main &amp; 2nd floors and the skylight are older units that are not very energy efficient. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



Savings: 

By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 417 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.0 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window (12 windows max rebate = $3,000) rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, an eligible exterior wall insulation upgrade is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA.  



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors>

There is an older hollow core, exterior wood door that exits the basement. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading this door to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified steel insulated door.



Savings: 

By replacing this hollow wood door with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified steel insulated door, you could potentially save $ 73 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for upgrading older doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil hot water tank, co-vented together with the furnace up a chimney. You may want to consider replacing your this oil hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 191 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.8 GJ and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 83.4% efficient oil furnace. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from the oil furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,604 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 41.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500   from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has 3 toilets and the one on the 2nd floor and the one in the basement are older toilets that should be replaced with a lower water consumption, yet high efficiency model (like the one you have on the main floor). When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing these toilets with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $12,250 in rebates from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $   500 for the exterior basement pony wall insulation

     - $3,000 for the windows

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $   750 for the air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate



NOTE: If you do not proceed with the wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebate from the above list that would still apply would be the heating system rebate, however that rebate would decrease from $3,000 to $1,500. So ............. in a way, there is a potential to receive up to $10,750 if you do choose to add a minimum R10 of wall insulation in conjunction with the windows that you want to upgrade.



In addition to the estimated $12,250 in rebates from the DHERP, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR washing machine - $200

     - ESTAR on-demand hot water heater - $1,250

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range downdraft. all 3 bathroom fans, the workout room fan &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 5.9 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and hot water tank co-vent up the same chimney (there is no dedicated outside combustion air supply in that mechanical room) so these appliances are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range downdraft fan (600 CFM) vents to the outside

        - All 3 bathrooms have an exhaust fan (the one on the 2nd floor allows alot of heat loss)

        - The workout room exhaust fan



You should consider replacing all the bathroom fans &amp; the workout room fan with an ENERGY STAR rated fans that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.38 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.03 per litre 

Propane - $0.62 per litre delivered, $2.46 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.09 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



